body:
[Thm 5 Hodge n point formula of arXiv:2609.02409, 'Membranes and Maps'] Suppose that $\sum_{i=1}^5 \epsilon_i = 0$. (conj:GW:vertex of the paper) holds if and only if, for all $n\in\{1,\ldots,5\}$, we have
    \begin{equation}

        \mathsf{H}(\epsilon_1^{-1},\ldots, \epsilon_n^{-1} ; \epsilon_1,\ldots, \epsilon_5 ; u)
        =
        \mathsf{V}(\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_n;u)
        \prod_{j=1}^n \frac{1}{u^2}
        \prod_{i\neq j} \frac{\gamfct{-\epsilon_j^{-1}\epsilon_i}{u\epsilon_j}}{\epsilon_i} \,,
    \end{equation}
    where
    \begin{equation}

        \mathsf{V}(\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_n;u)
        \coloneqq
        \begin{cases}
            1
            & n=1 \,, \\[0.75em]
            \dfrac{\varsigma(u \epsilon_3)\varsigma(u \epsilon_4)\varsigma(u \epsilon_5)}{\varsigma(u (\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2))}
            & n=2 \,, \\[0.95em]
            \varsigma(u \epsilon_4)^2 \varsigma(u \epsilon_5)^2
            & n=3 \,, \\[0.75em]
            \Big(\prod_{i=1}^5 \varsigma(u\epsilon_i) \Big) \, \varsigma(u\epsilon_5)^2 \sum_{i=1}^4 \kappa(u\epsilon_i)
            & n=4 \,, \\[0.75em]
            \Big(\prod_{i=1}^5 \varsigma(u \epsilon_i) \Big)^2 \Big(\big(\sum_{i=1}^5 \kappa(u \epsilon_i)\big)^2 -\frac{1}{4} \Big)
            & n=5 \,,
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
    and $\varsigma(x) = 2 \sinh \tfrac{x}{2}$, $\kappa(x) = \frac{d}{dx} \log{\varsigma(x)} = \tfrac{1}{2} \coth \tfrac{x}{2}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 63 (Integer-valued Newton basis) of arXiv:2608.29585, '2608.29585'] One has
\begin{align}
 \{f\in\Bbbk[A]:f(q^n)\in R\ (n\ge2)\}
 &=\bigoplus_{j\ge0}R\,\beta_j(A),
 \\
 \mathscr I_q^{\ge2}
 &=\bigcup_{M\ge0}A^{-M}
 \left(\bigoplus_{j\ge0}R\,\beta_j(A)\right).

\end{align}

[Theorem thm:rainbow of arXiv:2507.16188, 'The noisy voter model with general initial conditions'] Let $G = \mathbb{Z}_n$. There exists $c>0$ such that, for every $q\geq 3$, every $\theta\in(0,1]$ and every initial state $\mathsf{x}_0$,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{(2)}_t(\mathsf{x}_0) \geq 

\frac{c_0}{q^2}\, \mathcal{A}^{(2)}_t(\mathsf{x}_\mathrm{rbw})\,.
\end{equation}
Consequently, $T_{\mathsf{x}_0} \geq T_{\mathsf{x}_\mathrm{rbw}}+O(1)$, and thus $\mathsf{x}_\mathrm{rbw}$ is the asymptotically fastest initial condition. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:path-coupling of arXiv:2601.07045, 'From Continuous to Discrete: \\ a No-U-Turn Sampler for Permutations'] Let $G=(\mathcal{X},E)$ be a finite, connected, undirected graph whose edges
$\{u,v\}\in E$ are assigned lengths $l(u,v)\ge 1$, and let $d$ denote the
resulting shortest-path metric on $\mathcal{X}$. Let $K$ be a Markov kernel on
$\mathcal{X}$ with stationary distribution $\pi$. Suppose that for every edge
$\{u,v\}\in E$ there exists a coupling $(U,V)$ of $K(u,\cdot)$ and $K(v,\cdot)$
such that
\begin{equation}
\EE_{u,v}\!\left[d(U,V)\right]
\;\le\;
d(u,v)\,e^{-\alpha},
\qquad \text{for some }\alpha>0.
\end{equation}
Then, for all $t\ge 0$,
\[
\max_{x\in\mathcal{X}}
\bigl\|K^t(x,\cdot)-\pi\bigr\|_{\TV}
\;\le\;
e^{-\alpha t}\,\diam(\mathcal{X},d),
\]
where $\diam(\mathcal{X},d):=\max_{x,y\in\mathcal{X}} d(x,y)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2608.21255, 'Laws of the iterated logarithm for random Dirichlet series with general weights'] Assume that $\eta$ has a standard normal distribution. Then
\begin{equation}
\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{\log|\hat{Z}_n|}{\log n}=-1~~\text{{\rm a.s}}.
\end{equation}

