body:
[Lem lm:1356 of arXiv:2604.12963, 'Shocks, instability, and the twenty networks\\ of infinite geodesics in the Directed Lands'] Let $\omega\in\Omega_0$, $\theta\in\Theta^\omega$, and $x_0,s_0\in\mathbb{R}$. Then for all  $r<t$ and $x$ in $\mathbb{R}$, setting $a=I_{(x_0,s_0)}^{\theta-}(t)$, we have
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
&W^{\theta-}(a,t;x,r)\ge W^{\theta+}(a,t;x,r)\\
&\qquad\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\ 
\sup_{y\ge a}\{\mathcal L(x,r;y,t)+W^{\theta+}(y,t;a,t)\}\ge\sup_{y\le a}\{\mathcal L(x,r;y,t)+W^{\theta-}(y,t;a,t)\}.
\end{split}
\end{align}
Similarly, if we set $a=I_{(x_0,s_0)}^{\theta+}(t)$, then
\begin{align*}
&W^{\theta-}(a,t;x,r)\le W^{\theta+}(a,t;x,r)\\
&\qquad\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\ 
\sup_{y\ge a}\{\mathcal L(x,r;y,t)+W^{\theta+}(y,t;a,t)\}\le\sup_{y\le a}\{\mathcal L(x,r;y,t)+W^{\theta-}(y,t;a,t)\}.
\end{align*}

