[Lemma lem_gamma.from.K of arXiv:2411.07113, '\begin{LARGE}On differentiability and mass distributions of topologically typical multivar'] Consider $C \in \mathcal{C}_{ar}^d$ and let $\psi$, $\gamma$, and $F_K^d$ denote the 
corresponding generator, Williamson measure and Kendall distribution function, respectively. 
Then the following identity holds for all $z \in [0,\infty)$:
      \begin{equation}
     \gamma([0,z]) = \begin{cases}
        F_K^d(\psi(\frac{1}{z})),& \text{ if } z \in [\frac{1}{\varphi(0)},\infty)\\ 
        0,& \text{ if } z \in [0,\frac{1}{\varphi(0)}). 
     \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
Moreover, the non-decreasing transformation $h: [0,\infty] \rightarrow \mathbb{I}$, defined by $h(z)=\psi(\frac{1}{z})$ 
fulfills $h(0)=0$ and is non-singular in the sense that for every $F \in \mathcal{B}([0,\infty))$ we have that 
$\lambda(F)=0$ implies $\lambda^h(F)=0$.

