[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2605.11279, 'Flocking with Multiple Types: Competition, Fluid Limits and Traveling Waves'] Suppose that Assumptions \ref{assumption0}, \ref{assumption2}, \ref{assumption3}, and \ref{assumption4} hold, which implies that the sequence $\{\mu_n(\cdot) : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$ is $\CCC$-tight on $\mathscr{D}\big([0, \infty), \mathscr{P}_1(\real\times \{+,-\})\big)$, and that there exists a subsequence $\curlbr*{\mu_{n_k}(\cdot)\!: k\in\nat}$ that converges weakly to some measure $\mu(\cdot)$ in $\mathscr{C}\big([0, \infty), \mathscr{P}_1(\real\times \{+,-\})\big)$. Then for each $t\geq 0$ and $f\in \HHH$, 
    \begin{align*}
        A_{t, f}\big(\mu_{n_k}(\cdot)\big) \cdinf{k} A_{t, f}\big(\mu(\cdot)\big)
    \end{align*}
    in $\real$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

