[Proposition prop:u-expansion of arXiv:2606.25135, 'Convergence Rates for Semistochastic Processes'] Let $\mathcal{U}^t$ be a strongly continuous Markov semigroup with infinitesimal generator $\mathcal{L}$ and assume that $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_0 +\mathcal{L}_1$, with $\mathcal{L}_0$ generating the sub-Markov semigroup $\mathcal{U}_0^t$. Then the action of $\mathcal{U}^t$ on an observable $f$ can be decomposed into
\begin{equation*}
[\mathcal{U}^{t} f](x) =
[\mathcal{U}_{0}^{t}f](x)
+\int_{0}^{t} \left[\mathcal{U}_{0}^{t-s} \left( \mathcal{L}-\mathcal{L}_0 \right) \mathcal{U}^{s}f\right](x) \, {\mathrm{d}} s \ .
\end{equation*}

