body:
[1b 5 of arXiv:2506.09264, 'The bunkbed conjecture still holds for cactus graphs\\ and for graphs with certain biconne'] Let \(G\) and \(H\) be two graphs such that \(\vert V(G) \cap V(H)\vert = 1\). Consider two fixed percolation vectors \(\vec{p} \in [0,1]^{E(G)}\) and \(\vec{q} \in [0,1]^{E(H)}\) on \(G\) and \(H\), respectively.\\
Then, the statement that
\begin{center}\(\forall T \subseteq V(G\cup H)\)\emph{: } \(\forall v, w \in V(G\cup H)\)\emph{: } \(\mathscr{B}(G\cup H, T, (\vec{p},\vec{q}), v, w)\) holds\end{center}
is true if and only if both
\begin{center}\(\forall T \subseteq V(G)\)\emph{: } \(\forall v, w \in V(G)\)\emph{: } \(\mathscr{B}(G, T, \vec{p}, v, w)\) holds\end{center}
and
\begin{center}\(\forall T \subseteq V(H)\)\emph{: } \(\forall v, w \in V(H)\)\emph{: } \(\mathscr{B}(H, T, \vec{q}, v, w)\) holds\end{center}
are true. In particular, \(G\cup H\) satisfies the \emph{(}weak/strong\emph{)} bunkbed conjecture if and only if both \(G\) and \(H\) satisfy the \emph{(}weak/strong\emph{)} bunkbed conjecture.\vspace{6pt} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

