[Proposition prop:Rt of arXiv:2604.20327, 'Persistent Homology of the Wiener Sausage II: A Central Limit Theorem for Drifted Planar B'] One has
  \begin{equation}
    \sup_{t \geq 1} \mathbb{E}[R_t^2] < \infty.
  \end{equation}
  Consequently,
  \begin{equation}
    \frac{R_t}{\sqrt{t}} \to 0 \quad \text{in } L^2,
  \end{equation}
  hence also in probability.

