body:
[Corollary 7 (Helical direction and angle reconstruction) of arXiv:2609.00673, '2609.00673'] Under the hypotheses of the theorem,
\[
Q=T_i-x_iN_i+y_iB_i
\]
is independent of \(i\), and
\[
\boxed{
U=
\frac{T_i-x_iN_i+y_iB_i}
{\sqrt{1+x_i^2+y_i^2}}
}.
\]
Furthermore, if \(\alpha\) denotes the common tangent-direction angle, then
\[
\boxed{
\cos\alpha=\frac1{\sqrt{1+x_i^2+y_i^2}}.
}
\]
The formula chooses the orientation for which $T_i\cdot U>0$; the corresponding axis direction is the unoriented one-dimensional subspace $\mathbb RQ$.

[Lemma 12 (Monotone truncation) of arXiv:2609.03916, 'Halfspace Theorems for Anisotropic Minimal Surfaces and Perimeter Minimizers'] Let $U\subset\R^{n+1}$ be open, let
$v\in BV_{\mathrm{loc}}(U;\mathbb Z)$, and define
$\tau:\R\to[0,1]$ and $G\subset U$ by
\begin{equation}
 \tau(s):=\min\{1,\max\{0,s\}\},
 \qquad G:=\{v\ge1\}.
\end{equation}
Then $\tau\circ v=\chi_G$ almost everywhere.  There is a Borel function
$\vartheta:U\to[0,1]$ such that
\begin{equation}
 D(\tau\circ v)=\vartheta\,Dv.
\end{equation}
Consequently, as Radon measures,
\begin{equation}
 \mu_{T_G}^\Phi
 \le\mu_{\partial\llbracketset{v}}^\Phi.
\end{equation}

[Thm 17 (Main Theorem for $n=1$ and $p>0$) of arXiv:2609.00974, 'On Algebraic Integrability\\ of Vector Fields \\ with Rational Function Coefficients\\ tha'] We follow the assumptions and notations from Theorem \ref{Main Formula}. In particular, the prime $p$ is assumed to be big enough. The following are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\frac{\overline{L}}{P^p+L^p+H^p}=\mu \in \overline{k}$,
        \item One of the following is true:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item $L=0$. In this case: $\mu=0$.
            \item $P=H=0$ and there exists $\eta\in \overline{k}$, $\eta \ne 0$ such that each $c_i$ satisfies $(\eta x)^{p}=(\eta x)$. 

            In this case: $\mu \ne 0$ and $\eta^{p-1}=\mu$.
        \end{enumerate}
    \end{enumerate}

[Lemma l.jump_formula of arXiv:2411.14105, 'Simultaneous replica-symmetry breaking \\ for vector spin glasses'] Under~(i.setting of the paper), further assume that the decomposition is canonical and fix $X_0=0$.
If either $\overrightarrow{q}(s+)\neq\overrightarrow{q}(s)$ or $\overrightarrow{p}(s+)\neq\overrightarrow{p}(s)$ at some $s\in (0,1)$, then there are $t,t_\star\in\supp\mathrm{d}\alpha$ satisfying~(equation e.t,t_star_prop of the paper) and
\begin{gather*}
    \overrightarrow{p}(s+)-\overrightarrow{p}(s) = \frac{2}{t_\star-t}\int_t^{t_\star} \mathbb{E} \left[A_r^\intercal \left(\overrightarrow{q}(s+)-\overrightarrow{q}(s)\right)A_r\right] \mathrm{d} r .
\end{gather*}

[Definition eqn.Ht of arXiv:2411.15485, '\bf\Large Stochastic Volterra Equations for Local Times of Spectrally Positive L\'evy Proc'] The $\mathbb{H}^\#$-semimartingale $Y$ is {\rm standard} if
 	\begin{eqnarray}
 	\mathcal{H}_t:=  \Big\{ \sup_{s\leq t}|X_-\cdot dY(s)| : X \in\mathcal{S}_0,\, \sup_{s\leq t}\|X(s)\|_{\mathbb{H}}\leq 1  \Big\}
 	\end{eqnarray}
 	is stochastically bounded for each $t\geq 0$.

[Prop TVS-meas of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] {dim-TVS}}] 
    Fix $a\geq\lambda$, $u\in\mathbb R$. Denote $\mathds A=\mathds A_{-a,a}^u$. For $\eta>0$, define the random measures
    \begin{equation}
        \mu_\eta(dz) = \eta\mathrm{CR}(z, D\setminus\mathds A)^{-(\alpha_c-\eta)^2/2}dz.
    \end{equation}
    Then, for any $f\in C_c^\infty(D)$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{\eta\to0}(\mu_\eta, f) = (\mu, f)\in[0, \infty)
    \end{equation}
   exists as a limit in $L^1(\mathbb P)$ and
    \begin{equation}
       a(\mu, f) = \mathbb E[(:\! \cos(\alpha_c\phi) \!: , f)\mid\mathcal F_\mathds A].
   \end{equation}
   In particular, $(\mu_\eta)_{\eta>0}$ converge in law with respect to the weak topology of measures to a random measure $\mu$, which is measurable with respect to $\mathds A$ and supported in $\mathds A$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

