{"artifact_digest":"sha256:7d7f481201a387e47c00cf9770f7f102f100e78b735d143f98eeb8d5e4476d80","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/7d/7d7f481201a387e47c00cf9770f7f102f100e78b735d143f98eeb8d5e4476d80.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:338f54b0b73ffb337a49548a6a374b50b39cba246b643854aa4b3d64ac05f24e","bundle_entry_key":"2507-01236-theorem-11-wasserstein","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_040","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_040.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2507-01236-proposition-11-wasserstein","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Since $(\mathfrak{X},d,\mu)$ is asymptotically disintegrable with rate $(\epsilon,r)$, for all $n\in\mathbb{N}$ sufficiently large there is an event of $\mathbb{P}$-probability at least $1-\epsilon(n)$ such that $\mu$ admits a disintegration $\{\mu_i\}_{i=1}^n$ along the multifunction $F_{r(n),X_{[n]}}:\mathfrak{X}\rightarrow [n]$ defined by the balls $B(X_i,r(n))$. Conditioning on this event, the disintegration formula $\mu = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n\mu_i$ implies that $\lambda_n:=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (\mu_i\times\delta_{X_i})$ is a coupling between $\mu$ and $\hat{\mu}_n$; thus
