[Proposition prop:existence-universally-stable-sequence of arXiv:2608.15923, 'Universality of two-dimensional Markovian holonomy fields'] Let $\Sigma$ be a compact smooth oriented Riemannian surface, possibly with smooth boundary, and let $o\in\operatorname{int}(\Sigma)$. There exists a sequence $(\mathcal T_n)_{n\geq1}$ of smooth triangulations of $\Sigma$, with one-skeleta ${\mathbb{G}}_n=\mathcal T_n^{(1)}$, such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $o\in V({\mathbb{G}}_n)$ for every $n$;
\item every boundary component of $\Sigma$ is a subcomplex of $\mathcal T_n$;
\item ${\mathrm{mesh}}({\mathbb{G}}_n)\to0$;
\item $({\mathbb{G}}_n)_{n\geq1}$ is universally stable for regular loop families.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

