{"artifact_digest":"sha256:dd3ecf58625e8752ae5f4941c246924ddf9c96ba274c24ced30991f60f2340b6","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/dd/dd3ecf58625e8752ae5f4941c246924ddf9c96ba274c24ced30991f60f2340b6.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:60ad883c55a1421e70a029c71888bd317e12c2c05ad7a673a4641fb0a600930d","bundle_entry_key":"2411-13788-theorem-10-harn-ham3","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_106","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_106.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2411-13788-corollary-10-harn-ham3","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $t$ be fixed. For any $x,y\in \mathbb{R}^{m_0+\cdots+ m_r}$, consider the curve $$\gamma(s)=x+s(y-x), \ 0\le s\le 1$$ such that  $\gamma(0)=x,\gamma(1)=y$.
We have \begin{align*}
\sqrt{\ln\frac{C}{u}(y,t)}-\sqrt{\ln\frac{C}{u}(x,t)}&=\int_0^1\frac{d}{ds}\sqrt{\ln\frac{C}{u}(\gamma(s),t)}ds\\
&=\int_0^1\frac{\nabla \ln u(\gamma(s),t)\cdot\dot{\gamma}(s)}{2\sqrt{\ln\frac{C}{u}(\gamma(s),t)}}ds\\
&\le \int_0^1 \frac{\sqrt{\langle \mathcal{C}(t) \nabla \ln u,\nabla \ln u\rangle}\sqrt{\langle \mathcal{C}^{-1}(t) (y-x),y-x \rangle}}{2\sqrt{\ln\frac{C}{u}(\gamma(s),t)}} ds\\
