Assume $X$ is a $\sigma$-martingale. 
By definition \cite{JacodShiryaev2003}, there exists a strictly positive $\FF$-predictable process $\varphi$ such that $Y=\varphi\cdot X$ is a local martingale. 
By \cite[Proposition 2.5]{Zhunussova2026}, $\varphi$ admits the representation $\varphi_t=\phi(t)$ on $\{t\le\gamma\}$, where $\phi$ is a strictly positive, $\cB(\RR_+)\otimes\cH$-measurable function.

Since $X$ is stopped at $\gamma$ (i.e., $X_t=X_{t\wedge\gamma}$), the integral process $Y$ is inherently stopped at $\gamma$, yielding $Y_t=Y_\gamma$ on $\{t\ge\gamma\}$. 
