body:
[Prop 67 of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] The sequences $(a_n)_{n\geq 1}\in\R^{\N\setminus\{0\}}$ satisfying the formal identity
\begin{equation}

\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty} a_{n}
\dfrac{w^{n}}{(x-y)^{n}}
=
\sum_{n=1}^{+\infty} a_{n}
\Lambda_{n}(w,y)
\dfrac{1}{x^{n}},
\end{equation}
with the convention that
\begin{displaymath}
\dfrac{1}{(1-y/x)^{n}} =
\sum_{m=0}^{+\infty}
\dfrac{(m+n-1)!}{m!(n-1)!}
\,\dfrac{y^{m}}{x^{m}}
\end{displaymath}
are exactly the sequences of form
\begin{equation}

a_{n} = (-1)^{n-1}
\dfrac{1}{n!} a_{1},
\end{equation}
with $a_1\in\R$ being arbitrary.

