{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1b8dc4bbe28bbcd0d09269c59fb8b6069f3f46883114f8b4140d307d15375462","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/1b/1b8dc4bbe28bbcd0d09269c59fb8b6069f3f46883114f8b4140d307d15375462.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:6082557478d6d351c1863c4c46d4af69ead09d9f726d6b910fe39ab7fa1b3eb2","bundle_entry_key":"2609-02491-lemma-13-scalar-dr-variance","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_051","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_051.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-02491-lemma-13-scalar-dr-variance","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $H(x)\coloneqq x+\varsigma $. A direct calculation shows that $H$ satisfies the homogeneous equation
\[
H(x+a) = \frac{1}{x} \mleft( \int_0^xH(t)\,\mathrm{d} t + \int_\delta^{x+\delta}H(t)\,\mathrm{d} t \mright).
\]

Let $0<x\le y$ be sufficiently large. Multiplying~(equation eq:scalar_DR_equation of the paper) at $x$ by $x/y$ and subtracting the resulting identity from~(equation eq:scalar_DR_equation of the paper) at $y$, we obtain
\begin{equation}
