body:
[Defn 10 (Edge branching process $\BP_{\Sr, \ve}$) of arXiv:2605.21459, 'Network evolution with self-reinforcement'] Consider the following branching process: 
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)]
At time $0$, the population consists of $1$ individual $\tilde{v}_0$. This individual reproduces according to an inhomogeneous Poisson point process with rate function 
\begin{equation}

r_\ve^{\sss(1)}(t) = \frac{1+\delta}{1+\tfrac12\delta}\,(1-\eee^{-t}), \qquad t\geq 0.
\end{equation}
\item[(b)]

For all vertices other than the root, at time $t \ge 0$ after its birth, an existing (non-root) vertex $\ve$ reproduces according to an inhomogeneous Poisson point process with rate function 
    \begin{equation}

        r_\ve(t) = \frac{1}{1+\tfrac12\delta}\,(1-\eee^{-t}),\qquad t\geq 0,
    \end{equation} 
giving birth to a new vertex. We will sometimes refer to the vertices in this branching process as ``edges'' (towards a new individual).
\end{enumerate}
Denote the \emph{entire set of descendants}  of $\tilde{v}_0$ at time $t$ by $(\BP_{\Sr,\ve}(t))_{t \ge 0}$.

[Lem 33 of arXiv:2510.22783, 'Universality of Cutoff for Riffle Shuffling'] For each index $i\in [N]$ and for each $y \in \al, z \in \ar$, there exists a subset $A_{y,z,i}^{\stable}\subseteq A_{y,z}^{\stable}$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The size of the subset is bounded: $|A_{y,z,i}^{\stable}| \leq 2k$.
        \item We have
        \begin{align}
            \mathbb P\Big[i\in \bigcup_{y \in \al, z \in \ar}\bigcup_{x \in A_{y,z,i}^{\stable}} \cI(B_x)\Big]\geq 1 - e^{-\Omega(N^{\delta})}. 
        \end{align}
    \end{enumerate}

