with $B_{\mathsf{c}}(\mu,\delta)$ as given in the statement of Proposition \ref{lowerboundonballs}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma Cumulant expansion for epsilon cumulants of arXiv:2411.11341, 'Ultra high order cumulants and quantitative CLT for polynomials in Random Matrices'] Let $\mathcal{X}\sim GUE$. For any $r\geq 1$ the following holds
\begin{eqnarray}
&&K_r(Tr(Y_1^{\epsilon}),\dots, Tr(Y_r^\epsilon)) = \nonumber\\
&& N^{-m/2}\sum_{\pi\in \mathcal{P}(\pm[m])}\sum_{\substack{\tau\in \mathcal{P}(m) \\ \tau\vee\gamma=1_m}}\left[\sum_{\substack{\psi: \pm[m]\rightarrow [N] \\ ker(\psi)=\pi}}\mathbf{D}(\psi)\right]K_\tau(x_{\psi^\pi(1),\psi^\pi(-1)}^{(i_1)}(\epsilon(1)),\dots,x_{\psi^\pi(m),\psi^\pi(-m)}^{(i_m)}(\epsilon(m))),
\end{eqnarray}
where $x_{i,j}^{(k)}(1)=x_{i,j}^{(k)}$ and $x_{i,j}^{(k)}({\top})=x_{j,i}^{(k)}$.

[Lemma lem:stable-suspended-chart of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Uniformly in the prime tuple,
\begin{equation}
 |\mathcal P_h(\boldsymbol\ell)|\gg_t h^4.
\end{equation}
Every $A_t$ in (equation eq:iterated-prime-suspension of the paper) is a primitive
$(4+t)$-set with
\begin{equation}
 r_h(A_t)=t+2,\qquad
 \operatorname{ndiam}(A_t)=\left(\prod_{i=1}^t\ell_i\right)(ps+qr)
 \asymp_t h^{t+2}.
\end{equation}
If
$p_1(A_t)\leq\cdots\leq p_{t+2}(A_t)$ are its successive relation-birth
degrees, then
\begin{equation}
 p_j(A_t)\asymp_t h\quad(1\leq j\leq t+2),
 \qquad
 \operatorname{ndiam}(A_t)\asymp_t\prod_{j=1}^{t+2}p_j(A_t).
\end{equation}
Its projective coordinate ring is arithmetically Cohen--Macaulay of type two.
Moreover, if
\begin{equation}
 R=\prod_{i=1}^t\ell_i,\qquad
 J=j_1+\cdots+j_t,\qquad 0\leq j_i<\ell_i,
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
 |hA_t|=
 \sum_{0\leq j_i<\ell_i}
 \bigl(M_{h-J}-M_{u-J}+M_{v-J}-M_{b-J}-M_{c-J}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $M_x=\binom{x+3}{3}$ for $x\geq0$ and $M_x=0$ for $x<0$.

