body:
[Proposition 19 of arXiv:2607.08129, '2607.08129'] Let $T_1,\ldots,T_N$ be independent real-valued random variables, and let
$0\le c_i\le\beta$.  Fix an interval $J=(a,b]$ and define
\begin{equation}
 R_J:=\sup_{a\le u\le b}
 \left|\sum_{i=1}^Nc_i
 \left(
 \1_{\{a<T_i\le u\}}-\Prob(a<T_i\le u)
 \right)\right|
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 V_J:=\sum_{i=1}^Nc_i^2\Prob(T_i\in J).
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 \E R_J\le4\sqrt{V_J}.
\end{equation}
There is a universal constant $C_T<\infty$ such that, for every $x>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \Prob(R_J\ge\E R_J+x)
 \le C_T\exp\left\{
 -\frac{x}{C_T\beta}
 \log\left(1+
 \frac{x\beta}{V_J+\beta\E R_J}
 \right)
 \right\}.
\end{equation}
If $\beta=0$, both assertions hold trivially.  In particular, after changing the universal constant if necessary,
\begin{equation}
 \Prob(R_J\ge\E R_J+x)
 \le C_T\exp\bigg\{
 -\frac{x^2}{C_T\{V_J+\beta\E R_J+x\beta\}}
 \bigg\}.
\end{equation}

