for every $m=1,2$ and $t\in(0,\th_\ka)$ the following sequences of random variables are uniformly integrable:
\[\big\{\msf T_{\ka,\eps}(t)^m\mr e^{-mt\msf c_{\ka,\eps}}:\eps>0\big\}
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\big\{\msf M_{\ka,\eps}(t)^m\mr e^{-mt\msf c_{\ka,\eps}}:\eps>0\big\}.\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 3 of arXiv:2506.16260, 'Fractional Compound Poisson Random Fields on Plane'] For $(s_1,t_1)$ and $(s_2,t_2)$ in $\mathbb{R}^2_+$, we have the following joint moment:
	\begin{equation}
		\mathbb{E}\mathscr{X}(s_1,t_1)\mathscr{X}(s_2,t_2)=(s_1\wedge s_2)(t_1\wedge t_2),
	\end{equation}
	where $s\wedge t$ denotes the minimum of $s$ and $t$.

