[Corollary 4 of arXiv:2608.19832, 'Sharp Convex Concentration for Symmetric Random Tensors with Subgaussian Coordinates'] There are absolute constants $K_0,c,C>0$ such that the following holds.
Let $0<s\le c\sqrt n$ and assume
$\mathcal I_{n,d}(s)\ge1$. Then there are independent centered,
variance-one coordinates with $\psi_2$ norm at most $K_0$ and a convex
one-Lipschitz function $f$ such that, for every $m\in\R$,
\begin{equation}
 \Pp\left\{
 \abs{f(X^{\otimes d})-m}
 \ge c n^{(d-1)/2}s
 \right\}
 \ge\exp\big(-C\mathcal I_{n,d}(s)\big).
\end{equation}

[Definition of arXiv:2609.02848, 'The universal moduli space of non-degenerate $\Z/2\Z$~harmonic spinors'] The tame Fréchet space bundles ${\mathbf{E}}_{00}$ and ${\mathbf{F}}_{00}$ defined in (Prop_BundleOfAdmissibleZModTwoZSpinors of the paper) decompose into
  \begin{equation*}
    {\mathbf{E}}_{00} = {\mathbf{E}}_{00}^+ \oplus   {\mathbf{E}}_{00}^-
    \quad\text{and}\quad
    {\mathbf{F}}_{00} =   {\mathbf{F}}_{00}^+ \oplus {\mathbf{F}}_{00}^-.
  \end{equation*}
  with ${\mathbf{E}}_{00}^\pm$ denoting the \defined{universal bundle of admissible chiral $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ spinors}.
  The latter contains the open subset
  \begin{equation*}
    {\mathbf{E}}_{00}^{\pm,*} \subseteq {\mathbf{E}}_{00}^\pm
  \end{equation*}
  of \defined{non-degenerate} admissible chiral $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$ spinors.
  For every $({\mathbf{p}},[{\mathfrak l}];\Phi) \in  {\mathbf{E}}_{00}^{\pm,*}$,
  the map ${\mathbf{L}}_\Phi$ constructed in (Prop_AdmissibleZ2ZSpinor_LeadingOrderTerm of the paper) corestricts to
  \begin{equation*}
    {\mathbf{L}}_\Phi^\pm \in \Gamma\paren{Z,\Hom_\mathbb{A}(\overline{NZ},\check S_{\mathbf{p}}^\pm)}. 
  \end{equation*}
  The images define a family of local chiral residue conditions
  \begin{equation*}
    ({\mathbf{E}}_{00}^{\pm,*},f,{\mathbf{V}}^\pm).
  \end{equation*}
  If $\rk_\mathbb{A}\check S^\pm = 2$, this family satisfies the hypothesis of (Prop_TotallyReal=>UniformlyFredholm_Chiral of the paper).

[Def 43 (Backbone exploration) of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $x,y\in V_ g$. Let $
\in \Omega_{G_{ g}}$ with $\sn=\{x,y\}$. The backbone of $
$, denoted by $\Gamma(
