{"artifact_digest":"sha256:32409dd91a50fb3e558d10b48b8d62e481a1ed8a1fe1ae459897004de5f7767a","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/32/32409dd91a50fb3e558d10b48b8d62e481a1ed8a1fe1ae459897004de5f7767a.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:7c26a7069fad816c984764ab87213998bd7c5b923adf00c3cc40242bda988e27","bundle_entry_key":"2606-11248-lemma-11-dyadic-round-component-lower-b","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_072","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_072.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2606-11248-lemma-11-dyadic-round-component-lower-b","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: We use two classical inputs.

First, we use Le Gall's theorem on the areas of Brownian complementary components. Let
\[
M(u)=\operatorname{card}\{i\ge1:L_{i}>u\}
\]
be the number of bounded complementary components of $\mathbb{R}^{2}\setminus K_{1}$ with area strictly larger than $u$. Le Gall's Theorem~1.1 in [ref16] implies that there exists a deterministic constant $\gamma>0$ such that, almost surely,
\begin{equation}
u(\log(1/u))^{2}M(u)\to\gamma\qquad\text{as }u\downarrow0.
\end{equation}
We claim that this implies the rank-area asymptotic
\begin{equation}
