body:
[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2503.21737, 'High-intensity Voronoi percolation on manifolds'] Let $M$ be a connected, complete, $d$-dimensional Riemannian manifold satisfying the following conditions 
\begin{enumerate}[i)]

    \item $M$ has positive global injectivity radius \(r_\text{inj}(M)>0\),\footnote{See Section (sec:localcomparison of the paper) or [LeeRM] for a precise definition.}

    \item the sectional curvatures of M are uniformly bounded from above and from below,\footnote{see, e.g., [LeeRM] for a definition and an introduction to sectional curvatures.}

    \item $M$ is simply connected and one-ended,\footnote{We call $M$ \emph{one-ended} when $M\setminus B$ has exactly one unbounded connected component for any bounded $B\subset M$.}

    \item there exists a log-expanding, bi-infinite path in $M$, i.e., a Lipschitz continuous path $\gamma:(-\infty,\infty)\to M$ satisfying, for some constants $c,C\in(0,\infty)$, the inequality
        \begin{equation}
            \mathrm{d}_{M}(\gamma(t),\gamma(s))\geq c\log |t-s|-C ~~~ \text{ for all } t,s\in(-\infty,\infty).
        \end{equation} 
\end{enumerate} 

Then, both $p_c(M,\lambda)$ and $p_u(M,\lambda)$ converge to $p_c(\mathbb R^d)$ as $\lambda\rightarrow \infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition th-main of arXiv:2411.15401, 'The reference interval in higher-order stochastic dominance'] Let $a,b\in\mathbb{R}$ with $a<b$ and $n\in\mathbb{N}$. For $X,Y\in\mathcal X_{[a,b]}$, we have
\begin{align*}
&\mbox{(i) $X\ge_n^{[a,b]}Y$} \hspace{-0.3cm} & \iff  & \mbox{(ii) $\mathbb{E}[u(X)]\ge \mathbb{E}[u(Y)]$ $\forall u\in\mathcal U_n^{[a,b]}$} \hspace{-0.3cm} & \iff & \mbox{(iii) $\mathbb{E}[u(X)]\ge \mathbb{E}[u(Y)]$ $\forall u\in\overline{\mathcal U}_n^{[a,b]}$}   ; \\  
&\mbox{(iv) $X\ge_nY$} \hspace{-0.3cm}  & \iff  & \mbox{(v) $\mathbb{E}[u(X)]\ge \mathbb{E}[u(Y)]$ $\forall u\in\mathcal U_n$} \hspace{-0.3cm} &\iff  &\mbox{(vi) $\mathbb{E}[u(X)]\ge \mathbb{E}[u(Y)]$ $\forall u\in\overline{\mathcal U}_n$} ;
\end{align*}

and  (i) $\Rightarrow$ (iv) always holds true.  But (iv) $\Rightarrow$ (i) holds for all $X,Y\in \mathcal{X}_{[a,b]}$ if and only if $n\le 3$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:leading-field-convex-corner of arXiv:2609.00698, 'Kinetic Wedge Layers and Diffusive Limit of Neutron Transport in Polygonal Domains'] Let $\rho_0\in H^1(\Omega)$ be the ordinary weak harmonic solution with boundary data $e_0$, and suppose its two incident traces are sidewise $C^{2,\gamma}$ with the same value at $V_m$, written $\rho_0(V_m):=e_0(V_m)$. Let $\alpha_c$ satisfy
\begin{align}
 0<\alpha_c
 &<
 \min\left\{1,\pi/\omega_m-1\right\}.

\end{align}
Then there is $p_m\in\mathbb R^2$ such that
\begin{align}
 \rho_0(x)
 &=
 \rho_0(V_m)+p_m\cdot(x-V_m)
 +\mathcal R_m^{\rho_0}(x),
 \qquad
 \abs{\mathcal R_m^{\rho_0}(x)}
 +r_{m}\abs{\nabla\mathcal R_m^{\rho_0}(x)}
 \leq
 Cr_{m}^{1+\alpha_c}.

\end{align}
In particular $\rho_0$ and $\nabla\rho_0$ stay bounded at the vertex. No bound on second or higher derivatives is asserted.

