{"artifact_digest":"sha256:33bb1a58820e4674bbd4bd8fdd729e2bc1ba8bbfeb8bbc729aff06a48b25a0b0","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/33/33bb1a58820e4674bbd4bd8fdd729e2bc1ba8bbfeb8bbc729aff06a48b25a0b0.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:e8b26850f0d779d10f34d97ec2dbeb9d43a492a8bf2de8a79bd1802224e7617f","bundle_entry_key":"2609-02590-theorem-10-bilateral-gamma-scales","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_152","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_152.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-02590-theorem-10-bilateral-gamma-scales","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The centered positive Gamma component has cumulant generating function
\begin{equation*}
 K_+(t)=\alpha_+\{-\log(1-\beta_+t)-\beta_+t\},
 \qquad t<1/\beta_+.
\end{equation*}
For $0\leq x<1$,
\begin{equation}
 -\log(1-x)-x
 =\sum_{n\geq2}\frac{x^n}{n}
 \leq\frac{x^2}{2(1-x)}.

\end{equation}
Thus the centered positive component is right sub-gamma with its true variance
$\alpha_+\beta_+^2$ and scale $\beta_+$. Its positive MGF boundary is
$1/\beta_+$, so \eqref{eq:boundary-bounds} shows that this scale is optimal.

