{"artifact_digest":"sha256:b46977676f97e8d4127ddc40dd09be45f490c4fb3608b7e69f1abc8216efa684","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/b4/b46977676f97e8d4127ddc40dd09be45f490c4fb3608b7e69f1abc8216efa684.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:1886c3a0deeb4dc0e4192eaa5fb7931b0d630adb41240ece676dc3a63c2f8a21","bundle_entry_key":"2608-31043-lemma-3-truncation","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_071","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_071.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-31043-lemma-3-truncation","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Suppose first that $f\geq0$ is bounded and compactly supported, and
  since $f\in L^1\cap L^2$,
\begin{equation*}
 \mathcal{E}_s(u)
 =(2\pi)^{-n}\int_{\mathbb{R}^n}|\xi|^{-s}|\widehat f(\xi)|^2d\xi<\infty.
\end{equation*}
Indeed, the integral near the origin can be controlled by the fact $\widehat f(\xi)$ is bounded and $s<n$, while the integral on the region $|\xi|>1$ can be controlled by Plancherel's theorem. Thus $u\in H^{s/2}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ and for any $\phi\in C_{c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ there holds
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}_{s}(u,\phi)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^n}f\phi dx
\end{equation}
by the Fourier transformation. The scalar inequality
