[Proposition prop:primal-optimizers-finite-p of arXiv:2605.14149, 'The mean-field limit of online stochastic vector balancing'] Let $p\in[2,\infty)$ and $\delta\in[0,\infty)$, and write $q=p/(p-1)$ for the conjugate exponent. The optimization problems $V_{p,\delta}^{\infty}$ and $P_{q,\delta}$ admit unique optimizers $\alpha$ and $Y$. Denoting $X=X^\alpha$ and $Y(t)={\mathbb E}[Y\,|\,\mathcal{F}(t)]$, we have
    \begin{equation}
        0 < \|X(1)\|_p <\infty,\qquad \int_0^1 \|Y(t)\|_2\,dt <\infty,\qquad \|Y(t)\|_2 > 0\quad\text{for $t>0$},

    \end{equation}
    and the following duality relations hold almost surely:
    \begin{equation}
        Y = \frac{X(1) |X(1)|^{p-2}}{\|X(1)\|_p^{p-1}}, \qquad \alpha(t) =-\lambda(t)Y(t)\quad\text{for a.e.\ $t\in(0,1)$},

    \end{equation}
    where $\lambda(t) \in (0,\infty)$ is defined by
    \begin{equation}
        \lambda(t) \coloneqq \min\bigg(\frac{c_0}{\|Y(t)\|_2},\, \frac{1}{\delta}\bigg) ,\qquad t\in(0,1).

    \end{equation}
    If $\delta=0$, we interpret the minimum as $\lambda(t)=c_0/\|Y(t)\|_2$. Lastly, the function $t\mapsto \lambda(t)$ is continuous, and if $\delta>0$, it is bounded from above. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 44 (Initial estimate) of arXiv:2608.27307, '2608.27307'] Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:opt_ll}, let $E_*$ be a regular right edge in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:reg_r_edge}. Uniformly for $|E-E_*|\leq\eps_*$ and $\eps_*\leq\eta\leq1$,
        \begin{align}
                \norm{\calP[G(E+\ii\eta)-\Pi(E+\ii\eta)]} = O_\prec\left(\frac1{N\eta}\right). 
        \end{align}
        On the same domain,
        \begin{align}
                G(z)-\Pi(z)=O_\prec\left(\sqrt{\frac{\Im m(z)}{N\eta}}+\frac1{N\eta}\right),\qquad \avg{B(G(z)-\Pi(z))}_L = O_\prec\left(\frac1{N\eta}\right) 
        \end{align}
        uniformly for deterministic $B$ with $\|B\| = O(1)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

