[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2608.29554, 'Limiting Behavior of a Class of Hermitian Yang--Mills Metrics, II: Exponential Approximati'] There exist constants $c>0$ and $\epsilon_0>0$, independent of
$\epsilon$, such that for any $0<\epsilon\leq\epsilon_0$,
\begin{equation}
\|\Tr H_\epsilon\|_{L^\infty}
\leq
\left(1+e^{-c/\epsilon}\right)
\|\Tr H_\epsilon\|_{L^1(dV)}.

\end{equation}

[Defn eq:-19 of arXiv:2411.13403, 'Path weighting method for spot sensitivities and its variance reduction'] We denote by $\hat{Z}$ the vector obtained by reflecting the first
element of $Z$, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\hat{Z}=(-Z_{1},Z_{2},\dots,Z_{N}),
\end{equation}
and by $\bar{Z}$ the vector obtained by replacing the first element
of $Z$ by zero, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\bar{Z}=(0,Z_{2},\dots,Z_{N}).
\end{equation}
Moreover, the process $\hat{S}_{t}$ and $\bar{S}_{t}$ are defined
to be generated by replacing $Z$ with $\hat{Z}$ and $\bar{Z}$ in
((eq:-1 of the paper)) respectively, i.e. 
\begin{align}
\hat{S}_{t_{i+1}} & =H_{i}(\hat{S}_{t_{i}},\sqrt{\Delta t_{i+1}}\hat{Z}_{i+1}),\;\hat{S}_{t_{0}}=s,\\
\bar{S}_{t_{i+1}} & =H_{i}(\bar{S}_{t_{i}},\sqrt{\Delta t_{i+1}}\bar{Z}_{i+1}),\;\bar{S}_{t_{0}}=s.
\end{align}

[Theorem 62 (All-degree canonical Gaussian factor and raw-register maximality) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] The series \eqref{all:eq:4.7}--\eqref{all:eq:4.8} converge in $L^2$ and
\begin{equation}
 F=A^{\can}+G^{\can},
 \qquad
 G^{\can}\indep A^{\can}.

\end{equation}
Let $T=\bigoplus_sT_s$ be a closed graded subspace and define the Gaussian factor extracted
from the linear part of $F$ by
\begin{equation}
 G_{T,a}=\sum_{s\ge1}I_1(P_{T_s}\ell_{a,s}).

\end{equation}
Assume that its one-leg raw register against the nonlinear terminal vanishes:
\begin{equation}
 L_{s,\nu}^*t=0
 \qquad(t\in T_s,\ \nu\ge1).

\end{equation}
Then $T_s\subset\cN_s(F)^\perp$ for every $s$, and
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Cov}(G_T)
 \preccurlyeq\operatorname{Cov}(G^{\can}).

\end{equation}

Let X = A union B where A and B are both CLOSED in X (or both open, or more generally a locally finite closed cover). Let f : A -> Y and g : B -> Y be continuous and agree on A intersect B. Then the combined function h : X -> Y defined by h = f on A and h = g on B is well defined and continuous. The closedness (or openness, or local finiteness) of the pieces is essential: for an arbitrary cover by two sets the conclusion is false, as the cover of R by the rationals and the irrationals shows, where two constant functions paste to a nowhere continuous function.
The pieces must be finitely many closed sets, or arbitrarily many open sets, or a locally finite closed family;
and the functions must AGREE on the overlap, which is what makes h well defined.

[Corollary 16 of arXiv:2608.30304, '2608.30304'] Let $a,b\in[-\tfrac12,A_0]$ and suppose
\begin{equation}
1+x>\frac2{(n+1)^2}\qquad\text{and}\qquad 1-x>\frac2{(n+1)^2}.
\end{equation}
If $n\ge1$ then
\begin{equation}
F_{n,a,b}(x)\le\theta_L\bigl\{F_{n-1,a,b+1}(x)+F_{n-1,a,b+2}(x)\bigr\},
\qquad \theta_L=5.6 ,
\end{equation}
with $\theta_L=3.3$ if $n\ge2$; and if $n\ge2$ then
\begin{equation}
F_{n,a,b}(x)\le\theta_2\sum_{i=1}^2\sum_{j=1}^2F_{n-2,a+i,b+j}(x),
\qquad \theta_2=17 .
\end{equation}

[Lem 19 of arXiv:2603.28702, 'Distribution of the magnetization of the critical Ising model on sparse random graphs'] If $L\notin\mathcal{L}_0$, then
\begin{equation}
\SL{\substack{G_1,\dots,G_r\\ \text{correct}\ L}}T(G_1,\dots,G_r)=o\left(n^{r_1/2}\right).
\end{equation}

