[Thm expl_asymptotics of arXiv:2411.00629, '\Large Transformation of Stochastic Recursions and Critical Phenomena in the Analysis of t'] Let $\alpha>1$.  Then as $t\to\infty$, 

\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P (L\ge t)\sim e^{-t} \ \text{and}\  \mathbb P (S\ge t)\sim e^{-t}
\end{equation}

so that ${\mathbb{E}}(S)\le {\mathbb{E}}(L)<\infty$, where $f(t)\sim e^{-t}$ means $f(t)/e^{-t}\in[1/c,c]$ for 
all $t>t_0$ for some $c,t_0>0$.
 In particular, ${\mathbb{E}}(S)\le {\mathbb{E}}(L)<\infty$.

