[Proposition 21 (Finite-length inverse-system duality) of arXiv:2609.02449, 'Finite Directional Coefficient Modules over Euler Fibers of $A$-Hypergeometric Systems'] Let $N$ be a finite-length $S$-module satisfying $\Supp_{S}(N)\subset\{\mathfrak{m}\}$.
Then 
\begin{equation}

\dim_{\C}\Hom_{S}(N,\C[\bm{y}])=\length_{S}(N).
\end{equation}
In particular, $\Hom_{S}(N,\C[\bm{y}])$ is finite-dimensional.

[Proposition 34 of arXiv:2608.29157, 'Dilator-based analysis of $\KP$'] \pushQED{\qed} 
    Suppose that $L(u)_\alpha$ is admissible. If there is an $\sfu\RS_\alpha$-proof of a sequent $\Gamma$, then $L(u)_\alpha\vDash \llbr \Gamma\rrbr$.
    If $\Gamma$ has a $\sfu\RS_\alpha$-proof without using $(\mathrm{Refl})$ rule, then $L(u)_\alpha\vDash \Gamma$ regardless of the admissibility of $\alpha$. \qedhere (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 30 (Scalar quotient recursion and convergence) of arXiv:2608.30027, 'Under Ricci flow, a 3-torus goes flat'] Fix \(C_0\geq0\), and let \(b_k\geq0\), \(k\geq0\), be a summable
sequence.  Suppose that the sequences \(E_k\geq0\) and \(\ell_k>0\),
\(k\geq0\), satisfy
\begin{equation}
        E_{k+1}\leq \frac{100E_k}{1+99E_k/2}+C_0b_k,
        \qquad
        \ell_{k+1}\leq(1+C_0b_k)
        \left(\frac{1+99E_k/2}{100}\right)^{1/2}\ell_k.
\end{equation}
Then \(\sup_kE_k<\infty\) and \(\sup_k\ell_k<\infty\), with a bound depending
only on \(E_0\), \(\ell_0\), \(C_0\), and \(\sum_k b_k\).  If, in addition,
\begin{equation}
        \inf_k\ell_k>0,
\end{equation}
then there is a number \(\ell_\infty>0\) such that
\begin{equation}
        \ell_k\longrightarrow\ell_\infty,
        \qquad E_k\longrightarrow2.
\end{equation}

