{"artifact_digest":"sha256:65871b66a431771e0c55843d52a397739e1ea00bcb6e7bfa1e834d0f0884fda9","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/65/65871b66a431771e0c55843d52a397739e1ea00bcb6e7bfa1e834d0f0884fda9.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:d8a3c03a36d522499b69e9110a2338edf86c5696b38022a3dee1e87fe8e68d18","bundle_entry_key":"2511-09517-lemma-67-cdfi","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_005","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s1_005.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2511-09517-lemma-67-cdfi","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Recall the definition of $\Delta(n,k)$ in \eqref{def:Delta}. Tightness implies that for any $\delta > 0$,
\begin{align}
    \lim_{k\to\infty} \limsup_{n\to \infty} \p_n\left(\max_{v\in V(\T^n)} d(v,\{V^n_1,\cdots,V^n_k\})>\delta n\right) =0,
\end{align}
where $\{V^n_{i}:1\le i\le k\}$ is a uniformly chosen 
$k$-tuple of distinct vertices.

On the event 
